# PHASE 10 — WEBSITE PERFORMANCE AND SCALABILITY

Performance is a non-negotiable requirement.

The website may eventually contain tens of thousands of localized Markdown files.

Architect the application to support this growth without unnecessary browser-side overhead.

## Important Distinction

Markdown source-file count and visitor-facing website performance are different concerns.

Do not load all Markdown content into the browser.

Do not serialize the complete article database into client-side JavaScript.

Astro should render published content into static HTML at build time.

## Browser Performance

Prioritize:

- Minimal JavaScript.
- Static HTML.
- Efficient CSS.
- Optimized fonts.
- Appropriate image optimization.
- Responsive images.
- Lazy loading for suitable media.
- Small interactive scripts.
- Efficient search-index loading.
- Minimal layout shifts.
- Proper asset caching.
- Accessible and stable rendering.

Avoid large dependencies and unnecessary hydration.

## Build Performance

Separate build-time performance from browser performance.

As content grows:

- Avoid repeated full-collection processing in every component.
- Use shared content utilities.
- Cache or reuse content metadata appropriately during builds.
- Use efficient article queries.
- Generate paginated listings.
- Avoid unnecessary build-time remote requests.
- Monitor build duration and memory usage.
- Monitor generated page count.
- Keep search indexing efficient.
- Evaluate incremental build features only after confirming compatibility and reliability.

Do not adopt experimental features without testing them.

## Performance Objectives

Target strong Core Web Vitals:

- LCP: 2.5 seconds or better at the 75th percentile.
- INP: 200 ms or better.
- CLS: 0.1 or better.

Aim for high Lighthouse scores across Performance, Accessibility, Best Practices, and SEO.

These are testing targets, not guaranteed outcomes.

Verify performance using the available local tools and document anything that cannot be measured accurately.

Do not claim perfect performance without evidence.

